  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nWesterly flow is keeping a progressive yet mostly dry upper \npattern over Nevada. Currently there is a week upper level trough \nthat is moving through the Pacific Northwest, outside of slight \nincrease in clouds across northern Nevada most of the activity \nwith this system will stay north into Oregon and Idaho. However \nthere are a few models that do show that some very light showers \ncould brush far northern Humboldt county Tuesday afternoon. So, \nhave introduced a very low 15% chance for isolated showers for the\nfar northern border. Winds will be breezy Tuesday out of the west\nat 10 to 20 mph with a few gusts up to 35 mph possible, as the \ntail of the upper level jet will extend over NV. Clouds will \ndecrease again Wednesday as this system departs, but the broad \nwesterly flow will continue through Thursday afternoon. Attention \nwill then turn to our next major weather system that will move in \nlate Thursday and last through the Weekend. Models are showing a \nlarge longwave trough digging south along the wast coast, with up \nto three local maximums in vorticity, that will act as a focus \nfor the development of showers and thunderstorms, one off the \ncoast of Washington, one off the southern California channel \nIsland, and a third in the middle off of the SF Bay area. It is \nthis third area that will serve as a focus for Nevada, bringing \nseveral rounds of isolated to scattered showers, thunderstorms, \nand mountain snowfall through Monday morning. To be fair there is \nstill a lot of unknowns with this system, the first being is how \nare all of the vorticity centers portrayed in model solutions \nwill interact with each other. Second, how strong will these \ncenters be. Third, what will be the track of the trough. Models \nshow this trough moving as one piece despite the split nature \nmodels have shown in recent runs. Already models have cut \nforecasted QPF for Nevada almost in half from this morning, with \nmore swings expected before this system will be able to be sampled\nby observations. Right now multi-day precipitation amounts look \nto be about a trace to 0.25\u201d of rain for passes and valleys, with \nup to 0.50\u201d to an inch of water for the higher passes and \nmountains. Snow levels will again start high at 9000 feet, \ndropping to about 6500 feet by Sunday. With a trace to 0.5\u201d \npossible for the passes, and up to 1 foot of snow for the range \npeaks. Monday and Tuesday high pressure will build in as the \ntrough departs bringing quieter weather back into the region. \nTemperatures will be near to, to slightly below normal for the \nnest week, as high will range in the upper 40s to upper 50s, with \nlows in the mid 20s to upper 30s. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence in quiet yet breezy conditions Tuesday \nthrough Thursday with winds out of the west at 10 to 20 MPH with \ngusts up to 35 MPH possible. \n\nThere is high confidence for the potential for several rounds of \nscattered showers and thunderstorms starting late Thursday \nafternoon, and persisting on and off through early Monday morning.\n\nThere is high confidence in the return of upper ridging Monday \nafternoon through at least Tuesday of next week which will bring \nquiet, dry conditions for the region.",
